A||||21||batboy||March Batmobile updates||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||03-03-2000||09:33 PM||||I declared the month of March as work on the Batmobile month. I'm just going to use this topic to update progress on my project duing the month of March.<P>March 1: I used some epoxy putty like Fierodave mentioned and tried repairing my lower dash corner. My first attempt wasn't too bad. I still need to do a second layer.<P>March 3: I installed new Gabriel Gas Ryder front shocks. The entire front suspension and steering is officially rebuilt. Virtually everything except the rack is new.||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||03-03-2000||09:38 PM||||bryan, did you get my email?<P>i mailed the blue thingy off today!<P>enjoy... ding...ding...ding<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||03-04-2000||05:24 AM||||The fact that you've declared March as "Fix-everything-and-modify-the-rest-of-the-Batmobile month is amazing.  I've declared the same thing for the GT.  (Unfortunately, I've chosen March, <B>2002</B>!!)  Keep us posted on the Batmobiles' progress.||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||03-04-2000||09:37 PM||||March 4 (morning): I went into town and checked out the boneyard. They had two "new" Fieros, both silver 1985 notchbacks and both had four cylinder engines. Interiors of both are trashed, but I was able to salvage one good sunvisor from each car. I also got a front side marker lens that my 85 GT needed. They only charged me $4 for my parts...cool.<P>March 4 (early afternoon): Went home and installed my new old parts. Changed oil, greased the chassis, and replaced the plug wires on the 85 GT. Finally got around to working on the Batmobile.<P>March 4 (late afternoon and early evening): Spent the remainder of the day installing my front battery mounting kit. I had to carefully measure and cut a rectanglar hole in the plastic spare tire well in order to mount the battery box. The instructions say to check for anything in the way. Well, a brake line was smack-dab in the way, so I had to reroute that and reposition two of the line clamps. But, I finally got the new battery box in place and bolted down. I'll finish up this project tomorrow by running the wires and swapping in the battery. Yes, I've been taking pics with my digital camera and yes I'll write a "how to do this mod" article when I'm done.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-05-2000||09:00 PM||||March 5: Got some more work done on the battery relocation project. Ran into a couple problems, one of which was my own doing. I have a battery topic in the tech forum with the details and pics, check it out.<P>I took advantage of the beautiful weather and touched up several places with good ol' Rustoleum black spray can paint: the rear trim, the front raised "bumpers", and the mirrors. I masked everything off well, then lightly sanded the surfaces. The Batmobile is a go car not a show car, but that don't mean it has to look shabby.<P>I set the back end up on jack stands and yanked the wheels off. The rear control arms came off without too much trouble. But, I noticed the right inner CV boot is toast. Lots of play in the joint too. Guess I might as well replace that whole half shaft while I have stuff tore apart. Looks easy to work on without the strut in the way.||||reg||1
Z||000005||batboy||03-06-2000||11:27 PM||||March 6: I ordered a passenger side half shaft axle assembly today. I got a shipment of rear suspension goodies from RCC (see pic in the tech forum). After much grinding and drilling and beating with a hammer and punch, I finally got the rivets holding the rear ball joints out.||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||03-07-2000||11:36 PM||||March 7: I picked up the passenger's side drive axle and ordered a second one for the driver's side. I also ordered a pair of hifi axle bearing/seal combinations from the Fiero Store. I picked up my Trans Am hood louvers (which are rumored to be discontinued soon) from the Pontiac dealership. I got home and found a package containing a front air dam waiting for me. It looks just like the one on Sluppy's silver notchback. I burned the old rubber bushings out of the rear control arms, cleaned them up a little, and called it a day.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-07-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||SCCA FIERO||03-08-2000||03:11 AM||||Hey bat, how much were the vents?||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||03-08-2000||07:33 AM||||The Trans Am louvers were $22 each, kind of expensive for a couple little hunks of plastic. I couldn't find any of those suckers in the boneyards. They are actually supposed to be a three piece louver (top louves part, screen, and another piece), but I just bought the top part only. I've got a cardboard template made, just have to mark the hood and fire up the ol' saber saw again. Anyone who has cut into a hood before, feel free to give me advise before I mess it up.||||reg||1
Z||000009||bHooper||03-08-2000||01:25 PM||||did you get my package yet??  should be there by now.||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||03-08-2000||09:41 PM||||I'm dingy now, B.H. donated a blue thingy.<P>March 8: I ordered dew wipe strips (door window seals), picked up the other drive axle, and dropped off my rear control arms at the welder's shop. I pulled the rear tie rods, calipers, and rotors off. I actually considered replacing the rear wheel bearings too, since I was doing everything else, but the old bearings have no play in them and seem fine. They roll smoothly, so I'll keep them for a while longer. Here's a question: should I replace the drive axles next or the strut assembly?||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||03-09-2000||11:05 PM||||March 9: I received my battery terminal shipment from Crutchfield's. Working outside in the dark and cold, I finished removing the strut assemblies and the drive axles. All the rear suspension and axles came out much easier than I expected. Yippee!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||03-11-2000||12:45 AM||||March 10: I bought my special axle seal installing tool today for about 60 cents (went to the hardware store and got a foot of 2.5 inch O.D. PVC pipe). Picked up my rear control arms from the welder. I had them partial box in the bottom side with a piece of 1/8" thick steel plate for reinforcement against flexing. I cleaned them up a bit and shot a quick coat of black Rustoleum on them.||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||03-11-2000||10:32 PM||||March 11: The Batmobile is an automatic with Grand Am brakes, so I took all the e-brake cables out (I just had them wire tied up out of the way), it took way more time than I expected. My 4 speed 85 GT has a screwed up e-brake and I really want it fixed. Now I can pick out the best cables from two cars in order to make one good working parking brake system.<P>Today marked the turning point in the battle of the rear suspension. I've officially began putting it back together. I installed the ball joints and poly bushings onto the rear control arms. Well... it's a start. Today, I had to catch up on some neglected domestic chores and hang out with a couple neglected friends for a while too.||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||03-12-2000||08:09 PM||||March 12: I had quite the struggle getting the rear control arms back in. Those new poly bushings are very tight and hard to get lined up. After finally pressing the bushings into the arms a little bit more, and then liberal use of a hammer and a block of wood to line up the control arm bolt holes, finally mission accomplished. Next, I installed my new coil-over strut assemblies. The chrome spring sure looks good against the black strut and black wheel well and black control arm. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Plus it should add at least 20 horsepower just for the gee-whizz factor.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||DJRice||03-13-2000||09:30 AM||||Gotta luv that Rustoleum Horsepower!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Gokart||03-13-2000||08:17 PM||||Hey Bat, you've been able to work on your Fiero for 9 days straight now! HOW DO YOU GET AWAY WIT THAT! Your living out MY DREAM! I would give ANYTHING to be able to work on mine for a long stretch like that. Even if it was just cleaning/washing it! ||||reg||14
Z||000017||batboy||03-13-2000||11:17 PM||||Actually, out of the last 13 days, I've done at least some work to the Batmobile for 12 days (didn't do nothing on the 2nd). What can I say, the weather turned nice and I'm trying to make up for not doing much this last winter. Plus, I have no life!<P>March 13: Received my Fiero Store shipment of rear sway bar with poly bushings and a pair of axle bearings/seals (which are supposed to add support and stability for the rear drive axles). Speaking of seals, I had a bit of a struggle getting the old seals out and the new fancy bearings/seals back in. I just took my time and finally had success (using a big hammer and a PVC pipe). Gotta be very careful with those seals, you sure don't want them buggers leaking.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||03-14-2000||10:05 PM||||March 14: Got a UPS package with  two pair of dew wipe strips. Tonight, I installed the rear spindles, new rebuilt drive axles, bump steer correction kit, and new rear outer tie rod ends. Everything went together smoothly, a welcome change from struggling with the control arm bushings and axle seals. I still have to final torque everything and install the brake componants. Getting closer!||||reg||1
Z||000019||batboy||03-18-2000||06:44 PM||||March 18: The rear suspension project, except for the rear sway bar, is completed today. The weather has been all but nice lately. During a brief break in the weather, I torqued all the newly installed rear suspension down and reinstalled the rotors and calipers. I took a pic and the rain started failing again shortly after that.||||reg||1
Z||000020||batboy||03-19-2000||07:07 AM||||Follow this link to see the Batmobile's new rear suspension pic. It's also posted in another topic in the tech section.<BR> <A HREF="http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/rearsuspension.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://sites.netscape.net/batboybain/rearsuspension.jpg</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||03-20-2000||11:03 PM||||March 20: I received my new sail panel from Paul (Fiero Secrets). He sent me the panel before he even got my check. What a guy! I put the wheels back on the Batmobile and played around with the height adjustment on the coil-overs. Until I get my new tires and wheels, I like the looks of the lowest setting for now. I posted a pic in the tech section. Of course, I haven't driven it yet. I have to finish the battery project and then trailer it to the alignment shop first. I can tell the rear is WAY out of whack. Not even close, so no use driving it.||||reg||1
